### Pulling fresh `{{ target_branch_default | default('master') }}` from host
Your worktree fetches from a managed clone (`data/repos/.../<repo>.git`) that the host owns. When other beads merge to host master, the managed clone does **not** auto-update — it lags until either you push to GitHub origin (we don't, on every merge) or the host runs a sync. If your fetch can't see commits the host says are merged, the clone is stale.

Self-serve sync via the dashboard API — pulls host's `{{ target_branch_default | default('master') }}` into the managed clone you fetch from. The `{repo}` path segment is the dashboard worktree row's `repo_name`, not the container mount basename. For the main Autonomy checkout this is normally `autonomy`, even though the repo is mounted inside the container at `/workspace/repo`. Do not substitute `repo` just because the local path is `/workspace/repo`; if the endpoint returns `worktree not found: .../<session>/repo`, you used the wrong path segment.

```bash
DASHBOARD=https://{{ 'localhost' if config.network_host else 'host.docker.internal' }}:8080
REPO_NAME=$(curl -sk "$DASHBOARD/api/worktrees" | python3 -c 'import json, os, sys; rows=json.load(sys.stdin); print(next(r["repo_name"] for r in rows if r["session_name"] == os.environ["AUTONOMY_SESSION"]))')
curl -sk -X POST "$DASHBOARD/api/worktrees/$AUTONOMY_SESSION/$REPO_NAME/sync-base"
# Then your normal fetch+rebase will see the new commits:
git fetch <autonomy-root>/data/repos/.../<repo>.git master:hostsync/master
git rebase hostsync/master
```

The sync action is non-destructive — pure ref-update on the managed clone. Safe to call any time.
